WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.693
We present PL-MPC: closing the planning–learning loop.

2
00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:17.891
TD-MPC plans robot actions using a learned world model. The model predicts
action outcomes, while a critic estimates return beyond the planning
horizon. The robot executes one action, then replans.

3
00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:29.681
The policy proposes actions. Aligning it with the planner leaves three
issues: delayed reward propagation, uncertain terminal values, and
differences in episode quality.

4
00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:37.692
Our method modifies all three interfaces, retaining the world-model
architecture and MPPI optimizer.

5
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.759
Prior methods use one-step targets; we use hybrid multi-step targets.

6
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.692
Experience updates the world model, critic, and policy, guiding future
plans.

7
00:00:49.250 --> 00:01:03.008
In this illustration, one trajectory falls while the other survives.
Multi-step targets propagate later rewards sooner, helping the critic
sharpen the fall–survival boundary. Model predictions complete targets
beyond replay.

8
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:16.701
After crossing a hurdle, a planned trajectory may enter a less-visited
region. When target critics disagree there, ATE penalizes the online
terminal value, reducing reliance on uncertain estimates.

9
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:30.550
RAD gives stronger imitation weight to higher-return episodes, using
realized returns rather than critic estimates. Warmup delays this
additional loss, allowing the planner to collect useful trajectories
first.

10
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:43.109
On balance-hard, the best seed reaches fifty point nine percent success,
versus zero for the baseline seeds. The planner unlocks full-episode
balancing, beyond simply raising mean return.

11
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:57.205
On hurdle, the best seed reaches fifty-eight percent success, versus zero
for the baseline seeds. The planner clears successive obstacles and
completes the task—a qualitative change in behavior.

12
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:07.845
On other HumanoidBench tasks, performance remains competent, with
especially large gains on hard tasks such as balance-hard and hurdle.

13
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:18.688
In Isaac Gym, these representative wrench–nut alignment trials compare PL-
MPC and the baseline across initial object configurations.

14
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:30.897
The world model learned in Isaac Gym is deployed with online planning on
the real KUKA robot: zero-shot sim-to-real transfer. Contact requires
closed-loop realignment.

15
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:43.691
Additional PL-MPC trials show different initial configurations, including
an unseen object size. Planning uses the simulation-trained world model,
without real-world fine-tuning.

16
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:51.470
The same simulation-trained models support planning for two unseen object
sizes, with higher observed success.

17
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:58.095
Closing the planning–learning loop.
